A||N||14||Seanh||High Performance Internals for the 3.4L???||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-27-2001||01:37 PM||||Were on the internet can I buy good HP internals for the 3.4 Engine? I need forged rods and pistons, and a heaier duty crank...Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Primered 86GT w/ 3.4L HO T-6 turbocharged engine :)||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||10-27-2001||02:04 PM||||You'll have to get custom made forged pistons if you want forged pistons otherwise use Federal Mogul hypereutectic pistons. The stock connecting rods are forged! If you want stronger ones than get some SBC connecting rods and have a machinist narrow the big end down for you. There is no other crankshaft available for the 3.4-liter engine. The stock crank is very strong though. <P>Get the Chevy Power book and follow the recommendations it give you.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-27-2001||02:10 PM||||I would like to have forged pistons, but don't look like I can afford them. I found them for $600 a set, for a really nice set of forged, but can't afford that much. I need stuff that will hold up to 150-175 hp worth of NOS, so they will need to be strong.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-27-2001||02:22 PM||||Also, I didn't know I could use SBC rods on my engine...Please let me know more about this also...Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-27-2001||02:27 PM||||One more thing, 3.4's are only 2 bolt mains right?  I know they make such a thing as a 4 bolt main conversion kit to SBC, but is there any way to do it to a 3.4 block?||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||10-27-2001||11:13 PM||||If you want to put 175 horsepower worth of NOS on your engine but can't afford a $600 set of pistons than you need to rethink what you are trying to achieve.<P>175 shot of NOS on the 3.4 is doubling the horsepower of that engine.<P>You'll need ARP fasteners too. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||10-28-2001||12:22 AM||||are the iron head 3.4 and alloy head cranks and rods the same as the 3.4 twin cam or is that the best one to use may looklike eachother but may be diffrent steel alloys and or heat-treatsments as twin cam runs hi-rpm more of it's life and does make more power. or will DOHC crank fit in pushrod motors?????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||10-28-2001||01:42 PM||||bump<BR>will the twin cam crank and rods work in the pushrod motors?????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||10-28-2001||04:02 PM||||Yes it will because the DOHC and OHV engine share the same crankshaft and connecting rods. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cutlass207TDC||10-28-2001||08:43 PM||||Can the connecting rods of the OHV 3.4 actually handle the extra grunt of the TDC engine??||||reg||5
Z||000010||88formula||10-28-2001||09:31 PM||||If a stock 2.8 crankshaft can handle the constant pounding from the nitrous 1fst2m6 uses (ran a 12.9) than I would think so. I think the 60-degree v6 crankshafts are very strong as long as they haven’t been turned down too much. The weak links are the connecting rods, oiling system (which is different with the DOHC engine), and reciprocating fasteners.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierosound||10-30-2001||10:20 PM||||My 3.4L pushrod has 3.4L DOHC pistons, crank and rods.  They are interchangeable.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/tonys87.htm" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/tonys87.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||ray b||10-31-2001||12:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierosound:<BR><B>My 3.4L pushrod has 3.4L DOHC pistons, crank and rods.  They are interchangeable.<BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>same part #? same cost?? same alloy and heattreatment???<BR>will the dohc 3.4's better oil pump fit in the push rod motor???<BR>pistons give what c/r with what heads??<P><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||10-31-2001||08:19 AM||||I'm probally going to be doing a setup similar to your FieroSound, with a 3400 roller block. I'm wanting to use lots NOS though, so I don't know how well 3.4 DOHC internal will hold up any better then 3.4 OVH internals. I think I found some forged 3400 pistons for about $500, so it look like I have no other choice but to use those...||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||10-31-2001||11:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR>...The weak links are the connecting rods, <B>oiling system (which is different with the DOHC engine)</B>, and reciprocating fasteners.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The oiling system was updated in the 3.1L & 3.4L Blocks, the weak oiling system is only inherent in the older 2.8L blocks (1st change in '88).   Not that it makes much difference in this case.  <P>If you're serious about that much Nitrous, you'll definitely need to spend the dough on the Forged Pistons.  If you read up on The Nitrous info from 1FST2M6, you'll notice they recommend <B>not</B> using hypereutectic pistons: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006347.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006347.html</A> ||||reg||1
